ains required."}],"evidence":[{"sectionKey":"identity","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/182/overhead-slams/","sourceTitle":"Overhead Slams","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":78,"claims":["The standing overhead slam is an overhead-to-floor ballistic action with an explicit hinge, whole-body extension, downward release, and ball-retrieval rule.","Medicine Ball Rebound Slam to Catch must define support position, start and release path, force direction, rebound behavior, catch or retrieval rule, side order, and finish rather than relying on the generic word slam."]},{"sectionKey":"taxonomy","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/287/rotational-slam/","sourceTitle":"Rotational Slam","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":77,"claims":["A side-directed rotational slam differs from a straight overhead slam through its deliberate twist, lateral floor target, and alternating or single-side execution.","Classify Medicine Ball Rebound Slam to Catch by support position, bilateral versus side-specific execution, overhead versus scoop start, vertical versus diagonal floor vector, rebound requirement, and static versus dynamic entry."]},{"sectionKey":"anatomy","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/19826303/","sourceTitle":"Analysis of trunk muscle activity in the side medicine-ball throw","sourcePublisher":"Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":80,"claims":["Medicine-ball rotational output involves trunk musculature including the external obliques, with muscle activity and ball velocity affected by the task and ball mass.","Represent the feet, ankles, knees, hips, pelvis, trunk, scapulae, shoulders, elbows, wrists, and hands actually used by the selected standing, kneeling, straight, or rotational slam."]},{"sectionKey":"biomechanics","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/182/overhead-slams/","sourceTitle":"Overhead Slams","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":78,"claims":["The overhead slam uses lower-body loading and triple extension before a forceful overhead-to-floor action while trunk bracing and a hip hinge protect position.","Coach pressure from the floor through the hips and trunk into the ball, then redirect force into the assigned floor target without lumbar-dominant extension, flexion, or rotation."]},{"sectionKey":"difficulty","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37833510/","sourceTitle":"Effects of Upper-Body Plyometric Training on Physical Fitness in Healthy Youth and Young Adult Participants: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["Medicine-ball power exercises vary by position, task, dose, and population, so one generic category cannot represent their technical or physical demand.","Assess technical complexity, absolute physical load, coordination, supervision, failure consequence, impact, work-capacity demand, and overall difficulty directly. Athlete experience is programming context, not exercise-card classification."]},{"sectionKey":"load_fatigue_recovery","sourceUrl":"https://www.power-systems.com/pdf/PSystems_DigitalGuide_Jan18.pdf","sourceTitle":"2018 Buyers Guide","sourcePublisher":"Power Systems","sourceKind":"manufacturer_instruction","evidenceQuality":72,"claims":["No-bounce slam balls and rebounding medicine balls have materially different retrieval, catch, and repeat-cadence behavior.","Medicine Ball Rebound Slam to Catch must track ball mass and rebound type, high-intent slams, catches, side balance, technical speed loss, shoulder and trunk fatigue, floor contacts, recovery between sets, and weekly ballistic-ball volume."]},{"sectionKey":"constraints","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/continuing-education/certified/june-2019/7306/medicine-balls-an-ace-integrated-fitness-training-reg-model-workout/","sourceTitle":"Medicine Balls: An ACE Integrated Fitness Training Model Workout","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":78,"claims":["Medicine balls, wall balls, dead-weight balls, and slam balls are not interchangeable for every impact task; implement construction and rebound behavior must match the exercise.","Require an intact slam-rated ball, a floor rated for repeated impact, safe overhead and rebound clearance, stable footing or knee padding, athlete separation, and no bystanders in the impact, rebound, or retrieval zone."]},{"sectionKey":"dosage","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/pros/expert-articles/6941/five-exercises-to-help-your-clients-develop-power/","sourceTitle":"Five Exercises to Help Your Clients Develop Power","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":76,"claims":["High-velocity medicine-ball power work is commonly delivered in low-repetition sets with enough recovery to preserve output.","For power delivery, use roughly three to five sets of three to five high-quality slams or two to five per side with 60–180 seconds between sets; stop before velocity, target accuracy, posture, catch control, or safe retrieval declines."]},{"sectionKey":"instructions","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/exercise-library/182/overhead-slams/","sourceTitle":"Overhead Slams","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":78,"claims":["The instructional sequence must identify the starting stance, grip, lower-body load, overhead path, bracing action, floor target, and rebound or retrieval behavior.","State the ball type and mass, stance, target, permitted countermovement or rotation, catch rule, reset, side order, and exact rep-quality rule before the first repetition."]},{"sectionKey":"safety_stop_rules","sourceUrl":"https://www.nsca.com/globalassets/about/position-statements/position_stand_youth_resistance_training---2009.pdf","sourceTitle":"Youth Resistance Training: Updated Position Statement Paper From the NSCA","sourcePublisher":"National Strength and Conditioning Association","sourceKind":"professional_standard","evidenceQuality":88,"claims":["Youth resistance and power training should be supervised, technically appropriate, progressively dosed, and performed with safe equipment and space.","Stop for pain, numbness, dizziness, loss of grip, unsafe rebound, floor or ball failure, lane entry, uncontrolled catch, lumbar-dominant motion, loss of balance, knee discomfort in kneeling, accuracy collapse, or repeatable output loss."]},{"sectionKey":"programming","sourceUrl":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37833510/","sourceTitle":"Effects of Upper-Body Plyometric Training on Physical Fitness in Healthy Youth and Young Adult Participants: A Systematic Review with Meta-Analysis","sourcePublisher":"Sports Medicine - Open","sourceKind":"peer_reviewed_research","evidenceQuality":91,"claims":["Upper-body plyometric and medicine-ball training can improve power outcomes, while transfer depends on the chosen task and population.","Place Medicine Ball Rebound Slam to Catch early in Output work after relevant access and activation. Match stance, vector, ball mass, rebound behavior, and intent to the objective; do not turn maximal slams into indiscriminate fatigue work."]},{"sectionKey":"athlete_support","sourceUrl":"https://www.acefitness.org/resources/everyone/blog/3801/heavy-slam-ball-workout/","sourceTitle":"Heavy Slam Ball Workout","sourcePublisher":"American Council on Exercise","sourceKind":"expert_instruction","evidenceQuality":75,"claims":["Standing, kneeling, around-the-world, and side-directed slam versions need visibly different setup a